Definition of CFC income: Interest or any other income generated by financial assets; 
royalties or any other income generated from intellectual property; 
dividends and income from the disposal of shares, if these would be a subject to tax at the participating corporation;
income from financial leasing; 
income from insurance, banking and other financial activities; 
income from invoicing companies that earn sales and services income from goods and services purchased from and sold to associated enterprises, and add no or little economic value; 
The income of the CFC is to be determined in accordance with the Austrian tax law.

The CFC rules do not apply when:
- less than 1/3 of the total income qualifies as passive income;
- the CFC is a financial enterprise and its passive income comes for one-third or less from transactions with the taxpayer or with the taxpayer's associated entities.

When assessing whether a CFC carries out real economic activity, the Polish CFC rules recommend paying particular notice to whether: 
(i) the registration of a CFC matches an actual establishment intended to carry on real economic activities, i.e. the company has local, qualified personnel and the requisite equipment; 
(ii) a CFC has not been created as a structure functioning in isolation from economic reasons; 
(iii) the premises, personnel or equipment of a CFC are commensurate with the scope of its activities; 
(iv) arrangements concluded by a CFC are consistent with economic reality, have an economic justification, and are not manifestly contrary to the general economic interests of the company; and 
(v) a CFC independently performs its basic economic functions with the use of its own resources, including managers at the company’s location.

  		  An entity shall be treated as a controlled foreign company if the following conditions are cumulatively met: 
a) the taxpayer, alone or together with its associated enterprises, holds a direct or indirect holding of more than 50% of the voting rights or directly or indirectly owns more than 50% of the capital of the entity or is entitled to receive more than 50% of that entity's profits; 
b) the tax on profits actually paid for its profits by the entity or permanent establishment is less than the difference between the profit tax that would have been levied from the entity or the permanent establishment, calculated in accordance with the provisions regarding to the tax on profit from Fiscal Code of this Title and the profit tax actually paid for its profits by the entity or by the permanent establishment. For the purposes of this letter, the permanent establishment of an entity treated as a foreign controlled company shall not be taken into account in so far as the head office is not subject to taxation or is exempt from tax in the jurisdiction of the controlled foreign company.

Definition of CFC income: Interest or any other income generated by financial assets; 
royalties or any other income generated from intellectual property; 
dividends and income from the disposal of shares, if these would be a subject to tax at the participating corporation;
income from financial leasing; 
income from insurance, banking and other financial activities; 
income from invoicing companies that earn sales and services income from goods and services purchased from and sold to associated enterprises, and add no or little economic value; 
The income of the CFC is to be determined in accordance with the Austrian tax law.

DEFINITION:
Under article 185/2, § 1, Belgian Income Tax Code 1992 (BITC 92), a “CFC income” is defined as the non-distributed profit of a qualifying PE or CFC (as defined in art. 185/2, § 3, BITC 92) that must be included in the Belgian taxpayer’s taxable base (that are not excluded according to art. 185/2, § 4, BITC 92). It is computed by a 4-steps method detailed in article 185/2, § 2, BITC 92, which is summarized as follows:
1)	determining the CFC’s profit as if it were established in Belgium (art. 185/2, § 2, par. 2, BITC 92);
2)	limiting to the undistributed portion (art. 185/2, § 2, par. 3, BITC 92);
3)	limiting to the portion that is passive income (see list below) relative to the CFC’s total income (art. 185/2, § 2, par. 4, BITC 92); and
4)	limiting to the highest of the Belgian company’s direct percentages in voting rights, capital, or profit entitlement (art. 185/2, § 2, par. 5, BITC 92)
TYPES OF INCOME COVERED:
For step (3) above, as defined in article 185/2, § 2, par. 4, BITC 92, the “passive income” captured by the Belgian CFC rules includes, to the extent not treaty- or domestically exempt:
-	interest (and equivalent);
-	royalties (income from intellectual property);
-	dividends and income from the disposal of shares, bonds, options and similar securities;
-	rental income, including operational or financial leasing;
-	income from asset management, investment, insurance, banking and other financial activities;
-	income from the purchase and sale of goods or services to which the CFC adds little or no economic value.

The CFC rules do not apply when:
- less than 1/3 of the total income qualifies as passive income (art. 185/2, §4, par. 1, 2nd stripe, BITC 92);
- the CFC is a financial enterprise and its passive income comes for one-third or less from transactions with the taxpayer or with the taxpayer's associated entities (art. 185/2, § 4, par. 1, 3rd stripe, BITC 92).

The definition of CFC-income is in line with Article 7(2)(a) of ATAD, i.e.:

1. interest or any other income generated by financial assets;
2. royalties or any other income generated from intellectual property;
3. dividends and income from the disposal of shares;
4. income from financial leasing;
5. income from insurance, banking and other financial activities;
6. income from invoicing companies that earn sales and services income from goods and services purchased from and sold to associated enterprises, and add no or little economic value

Under the Danish CFC legislation, a Danish resident parent company is liable to taxation of the income (or only the CFC income) of an entity which is not subject to tax in Denmark, if: 

(1) The Danish parent company by itself, or together with its associated enterprises holds a direct or indirect participation of more than 50 percent of the voting rights, or owns directly or indirectly more than 50 percent of capital or is entitled to receive more than 50 percent of the profits of that entity;

 (2) More than 33% of the subsidiary's taxable income derives from CFC-income. The rules applies similarly to foreign permanent establishments.

CFC (Controlled Foreign Corporation) – a foreign resident company where all of the following apply:

more than 50% of the means of control are held, directly or indirectly, by Israeli residents;

more than 50% of its income is passive (as explained in the following questions);

the foreign tax rate is ≤15%; and the company’s shares are either not publicly traded, or, if partly listed, less than 30% of the rights were offered to the public.

Taxation of CFC profits applies only to controlling shareholders, i.e., an Israeli resident who holds, alone or together with a relative, directly or indirectly, at least 10% of any means of control, either at year-end, or on any day during the tax year and on any day in the following tax year.

Passive income includes:

income from interest or indexation differentials;

income from dividends, except dividends derived from income of a “held company” taxed at a rate exceeding 15% (a “held company” being a body of persons directly or indirectly held by the CFC at least 10%, or 5% if publicly traded);

income from royalties;

income from rental fees;

proceeds from the sale of a non-security asset, provided the sale is not classified as business or vocational capital gains;

proceeds from the sale of a security held for more than one year (“long-term proceeds”), even if sold within business or vocational activity under Israeli or foreign tax law.

Where a security is sold within less than one year, the capital gain is classified as passive income unless the assessing officer is convinced the sale constitutes business or vocational activity; the burden of proof rests with the taxpayer.

When assessing whether a CFC carries out real economic activity, the Polish CFC rules recommend paying particular notice to whether: 
(i) the registration of a CFC matches an actual establishment intended to carry on real economic activities, i.e. the company has local, qualified personnel and the requisite equipment; 
(ii) a CFC has not been created as a structure functioning in isolation from economic reasons; 
(iii) the premises, personnel or equipment of a CFC are commensurate with the scope of its activities; 
(iv) arrangements concluded by a CFC are consistent with economic reality, have an economic justification, and are not manifestly contrary to the general economic interests of the company; and 
(v) a CFC independently performs its basic economic functions with the use of its own resources, including managers at the company s location.

The following income derived from a controlled foreign enterprise shall not be included in the taxable income of the controlled foreign enterprise:
14-2.4.1. Dividends received from the controlled foreign enterprise;
14-2.4.2. Where the resident enterprise is a subsidiary of the controlled foreign enterprise, the portion of the consolidated profit of the controlled foreign enterprise attributable to the resident enterprise that is subject to taxation;
14-2.4.3. Income obtained through the permanent establishment of the controlled foreign enterprise in the Republic of Azerbaijan, as well as income derived from an enterprise established in the Republic of Azerbaijan, provided that such income has been subject to taxation in the Republic of Azerbaijan.

According to Article 14-2.1. of Tax Code of the Republic of Azerbaijan for taxation purposes, the profit of an enterprise that is not considered a resident of the Republic of Azerbaijan and is registered in countries (territories) with preferential taxation shall be subject to taxation in the Republic of Azerbaijan in the following cases: 
14-2.1.1. If a person considered a resident of the Republic of Azerbaijan, either individually or jointly with a resident or non-resident person with whom it is mutually affiliated, directly or indirectly holds more than 50 percent of the voting rights in the foreign enterprise, or more than 50 percent of its charter capital, or the right to receive more than 50 percent of the profit of that foreign enterprise;
14-2.1.2. If the actual tax paid on the profit of the controlled foreign enterprise is equal to or less than 75 percent of the profit tax that should have been paid on that profit in accordance with this Code;
14-2.1.3. If more than 30 percent of the annual income of the foreign enterprise consists of the following types of income:
14-2.1.3.1. Interest derived from financial assets;
14-2.1.3.2. Royalties derived from intellectual property;
14-2.1.3.3. Income derived from the sale of shares and equity interests;
14-2.1.3.4. Income derived from financial leasing;
14-2.1.3.5. Income derived from insurance, banking, and other financial operations;
14-2.1.3.6. Income derived from enterprises that generate income from goods and services without creating any economic value.

Where an entity or permanent establishment is treated as a controlled foreign company on the basis of the test the taxpayer shall include in the tax base the non-distributed income of the entity or the income of the permanent establishment which is derived from the following categories:
1.	interest or any other income generated by financial assets;
2.	royalties or any other income generated from intellectual property;
3.	dividends and income from the disposal of shares;
4. income from financial leasing;
5. income from insurance, banking and other financial activities;
6. income from invoicing companies that earn sales and services income from goods and services purchased from and sold to associated enterprises, and add no or little economic value

a.	dividends, except dividends received and/or accrued from controlled Non-Listed FC;
b.	interest, except interest received and/or accrued by controlled Non-Listed FC owned by resident Taxpayers who have a bank business license; 
c.	rent in the form of:
1)	rent received and/or accrued by controlled NonListed FC in connection with the use of land and/or building; and
2)	rent other than rent as referred to point 1) received and/or accrued by controlled NonListed FC coming from transactions with party that has a special relationship with the controlled Non-Listed FC; 
d.	royalty; and
e.	gain from sale or alienation of assets.

An entity shall be treated as a controlled foreign company if the following conditions are cumulatively met: 
a) the taxpayer, alone or together with its associated enterprises, holds a direct or indirect holding of more than 50% of the voting rights or directly or indirectly owns more than 50% of the capital of the entity or is entitled to receive more than 50% of that entity's profits; 
b) the tax on profits actually paid for its profits by the entity or permanent establishment is less than the difference between the profit tax that would have been levied from the entity or the permanent establishment, calculated in accordance with the provisions regarding to the tax on profit from Fiscal Code of this Title and the profit tax actually paid for its profits by the entity or by the permanent establishment. For the purposes of this letter, the permanent establishment of an entity treated as a foreign controlled company shall not be taken into account in so far as the head office is not subject to taxation or is exempt from tax in the jurisdiction of the controlled foreign company.
